// OnionHost Gate 4: ALL apt traffic goes through container U's
// client-only Tor, and only through it (DESIGN-v3 A4/A6).
//
// - tor+http:// sources are served by the method shipped in the
//   onionhost package (/usr/lib/apt/methods/tor+http), which speaks
//   SOCKS5 over U's unix socket. The stock method cannot address a
//   unix socket (measured: it silently falls back to a direct dial),
//   which is why the platform ships its own.
// - The plain http/https methods are disabled outright, following
//   the apt-transport-tor hardening guidance: a source added without
//   tor+ fails closed instead of leaking onto the clearnet.
// - No apt traffic ever goes via gateway A (it has SocksPort 0 and
//   carries the site's identity; update fetches must not touch it).
Acquire::tor::Proxy "unix:///run/onionhost/client/socks/sock";
// U's socket directory is 0700, owned by U's uid - deliberately:
// only root and U may touch it (the same reason the Dashboard probe
// goes through the root wizard-helper). apt's download sandbox user
// (_apt) could not even traverse that directory, so fetches run as
// root inside the root-owned updater. The method only ever writes
// into apt's own cache directories.
APT::Sandbox::User "root";
Dir::Bin::Methods::http "false";
Dir::Bin::Methods::https "false";
// Update fetches need no translations; skipping them halves Tor
// traffic and leaks nothing about the owner's languages.
Acquire::Languages "none";
Acquire::Retries "3";
